      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Role of X-ray Crystallography</strong><br>\nX-ray crystallography was a critical technique in uncovering the structure of DNA. Rosalind Franklin's X-ray diffraction images, which showed a clear X-shape, indicated a helical structure. These images were shown to Watson without Franklin's permission, which contributed to the development of the double helix model. Despite the controversy, the use of X-ray crystallography was a pioneering method for studying molecular structures.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine</strong><br>\nIn 1962, Watson, Crick, and Maurice Wilkins were awarded the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for their work on the molecular structure of nucleic acids and its significance for information transfer in living material. Unfortunately, Rosalind Franklin had passed away by then and was not eligible for the Nobel, which is not awarded posthumously.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Significance of the Discovery</strong><br>\nThe discovery of the DNA double helix was a monumental event in biology and medicine. It not only provided the fundamental understanding of how genetic information is stored and replicated but also paved the way for new scientific fields, including molecular biology, genetic engineering, and biotechnology.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
