          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Ethical Issues and DNA</strong><br>\nThe understanding of DNA's structure has led to significant ethical questions and debates. Issues such as genetic privacy, cloning, and gene therapy are central to current discussions in science and society. The potential to alter the genetic makeup of organisms, including humans, presents both promising medical applications and important ethical considerations.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Advances in Genetics</strong><br>\nSince Watson and Crick's discovery, advances in genetics have been rapid and transformative. The completion of the Human Genome Project in 2003, which provided a map of all the genes in humans, was a direct result of understanding the DNA structure. This project has facilitated breakthroughs in diagnosing, treating, and preventing disease.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Education and Legacy</strong><br>\nWatson and Crick's work has profoundly affected education, with genetics becoming a core part of the science curriculum in schools worldwide. Their discovery is a testament to the collaborative nature of scientific research and the enduring legacy of curiosity-driven discovery. Future generations of students continue to learn about their significant contribution to science and the lasting impact of their work on society.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
